## Your rights at work

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, osteoarthritis can count as a disability if it has a **substantial** and **long-term** effect on your daily life. Long-term means it has lasted, or is likely to last, 12 months or more. If it counts, your employer must consider **reasonable adjustments**, such as:

- a **suitable chair**, a sit-stand desk or a footrest
- **less standing, walking or lifting**
- a **parking space** near the entrance
- **flexible hours**, or time off for appointments and physiotherapy
- working from a **ground floor** office
- voice software if typing hurts your hands

**Access to Work** is a government grant that can pay for equipment, taxis to work, or a support worker. It does not have to be paid back.

## Benefits you may get

Benefits like PIP depend on **how osteoarthritis affects you**, not on the diagnosis. Osteoarthritis can affect many of the PIP activities, for example:

- **preparing food:** standing at the cooker, lifting pans, chopping with sore hands
- **washing:** getting in and out of the bath, washing your feet or hair
- **dressing:** putting on socks, shoes and trousers
- **using the toilet:** getting up from a low seat
- **moving around:** how far you can walk, reliably and repeatedly, without severe pain

Remember the rules: think about **most days**, and only count something as possible if you can do it **safely, well enough, as often as you need to and in a reasonable time**.

| Your age | Benefit to look at |
| --- | --- |
| 16 to State Pension age | [Personal Independence Payment (PIP)](/benefits/pip/) |
| State Pension age or over | [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) |

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

## Other help

- **Blue Badge:** you may be able to get one if you have great difficulty walking. In England you usually get one automatically if you score 8 points or more in the PIP "moving around" activity.
- **Equipment and home changes:** ask your council for a needs assessment. A **Disabled Facilities Grant** can help pay for bigger changes, like a stairlift or a level-access shower.
- **If you cannot work:** you may be able to get Universal Credit or New Style Employment and Support Allowance. Free advisers, such as Citizens Advice, can help you check.
